# issuewatch -- personal configuration
#
# Copy to ~/.config/issuewatch/config and edit. Nothing here needs a rebuild;
# everything else (batch size, context window, timeouts, score thresholds)
# stays in src/config.h, where it is tuned against a measured corpus.
#
#     install -Dm600 src/config.example ~/.config/issuewatch/config
#
# chmod 600 matters: this file holds your ntfy topic, and on ntfy.sh the topic
# name IS the authentication. Anyone who can read it can push to your phone.
#
# Syntax: one setting per line, "# " starts a comment, blank lines ignored.
# An unknown setting is an error rather than a silent no-op -- a typo that did
# nothing would leave you watching this list instead of yours.
#
# Any section you leave out keeps its built-in default. Any section you use
# REPLACES the default entirely, so to drop one repo, list the others.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- credentials
#
# ntfy-topic: `openssl rand -hex 16`. Then subscribe to that exact name in the
# ntfy app on your phone. Treat it like a password; there is no other auth.
#
# gist-id: create the gist once, seeded under the name the publish replaces:
#     printf '# issuewatch\n' > /tmp/issuewatch-board.md
#     gh gist create -d issuewatch /tmp/issuewatch-board.md
# then take the hex id from the URL it prints. GH_TOKEN must be a classic
# token with the `gist` scope; a fine-grained PAT cannot write gists.

ntfy-topic  REPLACE_ME_WITH_RANDOM_HEX
gist-id     REPLACE_ME_WITH_GIST_ID


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- repos
#
# One per line, owner/name. These are polled every cycle, and one of them is
# additionally back-swept per cycle to reach issues older than the watermark.
#
# Deliberately absent: llvm/llvm-project. At ~39k open issues it would dominate
# the backfill rotation for weeks and starve everything else.

repo tenstorrent/tt-metal
repo tenstorrent/tt-blacksmith
repo tinygrad/tinygrad
repo exo-explore/exo
repo ggml-org/llama.cpp
repo ggml-org/whisper.cpp
repo openssl/openssl
repo pytorch/pytorch
repo triton-lang/triton
repo NVIDIA/cutlass
repo NVIDIA/cccl
repo Dao-AILab/flash-attention
repo vllm-project/vllm
repo ROCm/composable_kernel
repo apache/tvm
repo opencv/opencv


# -------------------------------------------------------------------- profile
#
# Spliced into the LLM system prompt as the developer description. One line
# here is one line of the prompt, so the numbered structure is load-bearing:
# the ranking is what stops the model collapsing "paid", "reputation" and
# "interesting" into a single "is there money attached".
#
# Keep it short. It goes into EVERY request and competes with the issues
# themselves for OLLAMA_NUM_CTX; the daemon warns at startup if it gets large
# enough to push issues out of the context window.

profile C/C++ and CUDA developer: HPC, GPU kernels, compilers, systems. Rank candidates by, in order of weight:
profile (1) PAID. An explicit cash bounty, prize, grant or stipend, and nobody holding it yet -- a pull request only counts when you hold the assignment, so a claimed bounty is worth little. Outranks everything below it; the `payment:` line of each issue settles whether it applies.
profile (2) REPUTATION. Unpaid work in a high-visibility project where landing a patch is durable credibility: kernel or compiler internals, a correctness fix with a regression test, a measurable performance win. Worth 6-8 with no money attached, but earn it on the three marks -- a high-visibility repository is not by itself a reason to score high.
profile (3) INTEREST. Genuinely novel or difficult problems -- unusual hardware, emulators, numerical analysis, allocator and memory-model work -- that are worth doing for their own sake. Worth 6-7, same condition.
profile Strong topical signals: numerical correctness (fp32/fp16 accuracy, overflow, NaN, rounding, INT_MIN edge cases), kernel and matmul optimisation, tensor cores, memory/allocator bugs, race conditions, undefined behaviour, Linux build and toolchain breakage.
profile Ignore: docs and typos, translations, dependency bumps, Windows- or macOS-only problems, JS/Python packaging, support questions, and feature requests with no design attached. An issue that is merely a bug report with no route to a fix is not interesting either.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- keywords
#
# The prefilter, which runs BEFORE the LLM: an issue scoring under KW_SCORE_MIN
# is dropped without ever being judged. That is why these travel with the
# profile -- change one and you must change the other, or you will get an empty
# board with nothing in the log to explain it.
#
#     keyword       <weight> <term...>    matched in title, body and labels
#     label-keyword <weight> <term...>    matched in labels only
#
# The term is the rest of the line, so phrases need no quoting. Matching is
# case-insensitive literal substring with no word boundaries, which is why
# short tokens are avoided: "nan" would fire inside "maintenance".
#
# Weights may be negative, and that is not the same as a low weight: -10 pushes
# a bot issue below the gate rather than merely failing to lift it above.
# Label hits count KW_LABEL_MULTIPLIER times, capped at KW_COUNT_CAP per term.

keyword 24 bounty
keyword 24 gsoc
keyword 24 stipend
keyword 24 prize
keyword 3 reward
label-keyword 12 good first issue
label-keyword 10 help wanted
keyword 8 mentorship
keyword 4 performance
keyword 5 cuda
keyword 3 kernel
keyword 4 numerical
keyword 4 precision
keyword 4 overflow
keyword 4 rounding
keyword 4 fp32
keyword 3 fp16
keyword 4 tensor core
keyword 4 matmul
keyword 4 allocator
keyword 3 emulator
keyword 3 throughput
keyword 3 optimization
keyword 3 optimisation
keyword 3 sanitizer
keyword 4 use-after-free
keyword 4 data race
keyword 4 race condition
keyword 4 memory leak
keyword 4 segfault
keyword 3 regression
keyword 4 undefined behavior
keyword -10 dependabot
keyword -10 [bot]
keyword -8 bump version
keyword -6 typo
keyword -6 translation
keyword -5 changelog
keyword -4 readme
